{"slide_deck_markdown": "[
  {
    "slide_number": 1,
    "title": "Understanding Torque: The Twist That Makes Things Turn",
    "bullets": [
      "Torque is the rotational equivalent of force, causing objects to **rotate or twist**.",
      "It's crucial for understanding how levers, wrenches, and even planets move.",
      "Without torque, objects would only move in straight lines or remain still."
    ]
  },
  {
    "slide_number": 2,
    "title": "Calculating Torque: Force, Distance, and Angle Matter",
    "bullets": [
      "The formula for torque is **Ï = rF sin Î¸**.",
      "**r** is the distance from the pivot point to where the force is applied (lever arm).",
      "**F** is the magnitude of the applied force.",
      "**Î¸** is the angle between the force vector and the lever arm.",
      "Maximum torque occurs when the force is applied **perpendicular (sin 90Â° = 1)** to the lever arm."
    ]
  },
  {
    "slide_number": 3,
    "title": "Example 1: Loosening a Bolt with a Wrench",
    "bullets": [
      "Imagine a wrench with a **0.3 m** handle (r = 0.3 m).",
      "You apply a force of **100 N** at the end of the handle.",
      "If you push **perpendicularly** to the wrench (Î¸ = 90Â°), the torque is:",
      "Ï = (0.3 m) * (100 N) * sin(90Â°) = **30 Nm**.",
      "What if you pushed at a **45Â° angle**? Ï = (0.3 m) * (100 N) * sin(45Â°) â **21.2 Nm**.",
      "Why is it harder to loosen the bolt at an angle?"
    ]
  },
  {
    "slide_number": 4,
    "title": "Torque and Angular Acceleration: The Cause and Effect",
    "bullets": [
      "Just as force causes linear acceleration, torque causes **angular acceleration**.",
      "The relationship is given by **Ï = IÎ±**.",
      "**I** is the moment of inertia, representing an object's resistance to angular acceleration.",
      "**Î±** is the angular acceleration (how quickly the rotation speed changes)."
    ]
  },
  {
    "slide_number": 5,
    "title": "Moment of Inertia: Resistance to Rotation",
    "bullets": [
      "Moment of inertia (I) depends on an object's **mass distribution** and the **axis of rotation**.",
      "A mass further from the axis has a **greater moment of inertia**.",
      "Think of a figure skater: pulling arms in **decreases I**, leading to faster spin (higher Î± for same Ï)."
    ]
  },
  {
    "slide_number": 6,
    "title": "The Parallel Axis Theorem: Shifting the Pivot",
    "bullets": [
      "When the axis of rotation is **not through the center of mass**, the moment of inertia changes.",
      "The Parallel Axis Theorem states: **I = I_cm + MdÂ²**.",
      "**I_cm** is the moment of inertia about an axis through the center of mass.",
      "**M** is the total mass of the object.",
      "**d** is the perpendicular distance between the two parallel axes."
    ]
  },
  {
    "slide_number": 7,
    "title": "Example 2: Swinging a Door vs. a Rod",
    "bullets": [
      "Consider a uniform rod (like a door) of mass M and length L.",
      "If it rotates about its center (I_cm = MLÂ²/12), it's easy to spin.",
      "If it rotates about one end (like a door on hinges), the axis is **d = L/2** from the center.",
      "Using the Parallel Axis Theorem: I = MLÂ²/12 + M(L/2)Â² = MLÂ²/12 + MLÂ²/4 = **MLÂ²/3**.",
      "This **larger moment of inertia** means it takes more torque to achieve the same angular acceleration when rotating around an end."
    ]
  },
  {
    "slide_number": 8,
    "title": "Key Takeaways: Mastering Rotational Motion",
    "bullets": [
      "Torque (Ï = rF sin Î¸) is the **rotational force** that causes objects to turn.",
      "The **angle and distance** of force application are critical for effective torque.",
      "Torque directly relates to **angular acceleration** through moment of inertia (Ï = IÎ±).",
      "Moment of inertia (I) quantifies an object's **resistance to changes in rotational motion**.",
      "The **Parallel Axis Theorem** helps calculate I when the rotation axis is offset from the center of mass (I = I_cm + MdÂ²)."
    ]
  }
]"}

