pred_slide_deck_markdown:
[
  {
    "slide_number": 1,
    "title": "Photosynthesis: Capturing Light Energy",
    "bullets": [
      "Photosynthesis is the process plants use to convert light energy into chemical energy.",
      "It occurs primarily in **chloroplasts**, specialized organelles within plant cells.",
      "The key pigment, **chlorophyll**, absorbs light energy to power the reactions."
    ]
  },
  {
    "slide_number": 2,
    "title": "The Light-Dependent Reactions: ATP and NADPH Production",
    "bullets": [
      "The **light reactions** take place in the **grana**, stacks of thylakoids within chloroplasts.",
      "Light energy is captured and converted into two crucial energy carriers:",
      "â **ATP** (adenosine triphosphate), an energy currency for cellular processes.",
      "â **NADPH** (nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide phosphate), an electron carrier."
    ]
  },
  {
    "slide_number": 3,
    "title": "The Calvin Cycle: Fixing Carbon Dioxide",
    "bullets": [
      "The **Calvin cycle** (light-independent reactions) occurs in the **stroma**, the fluid-filled space of the chloroplast.",
      "Here, **carbon dioxide (CO2)** from the atmosphere is 'fixed' into organic molecules.",
      "This process utilizes the **ATP and NADPH** generated during the light reactions to build sugars."
    ]
  },
  {
    "slide_number": 4,
    "title": "Cellular Respiration: Gas Exchange Through Stomata",
    "bullets": [
      "Plants regulate gas exchange with the atmosphere through small pores called **stomata**.",
      "**CO2 enters** the plant for photosynthesis, while **O2 is released** as a byproduct.",
      "Stomata also play a role in water regulation, opening and closing to balance gas exchange and water loss."
    ]
  },
  {
    "slide_number": 5,
    "title": "Cell Division: Mitosis vs. Meiosis",
    "bullets": [
      "**Mitosis** is a type of cell division for **growth and repair**, producing two identical daughter cells.",
      "â Daughter cells have the **same number of chromosomes** as the parent cell.",
      "**Meiosis** is a specialized division for producing **gametes** (sex cells), like sperm and egg.",
      "â It **halves the chromosome number**, ensuring genetic diversity in offspring."
    ]
  },
  {
    "slide_number": 6,
    "title": "Conclusion: Key Processes for Life",
    "bullets": [
      "**Photosynthesis** fuels life by converting light into chemical energy, producing sugars and oxygen.",
      "**ATP and NADPH** are the critical energy carriers linking light reactions to carbon fixation.",
      "**Stomata** are vital for gas exchange, balancing CO2 intake and O2 release.",
      "**Mitosis** ensures growth and repair, while **meiosis** creates genetic diversity for reproduction."
    ]
  }
]

