      "It's composed of four nucleotide bases: **Adenine (A), Thymine (T), Guanine (G), and Cytosine (C)**.",
      "Crucially, A always pairs with **T**, and G always pairs with **C** â this is fundamental to genetic information."
    ]
  },
  {
    "slide_number": 2,
    "title": "DNA Replication: Passing on the Code",
    "bullets": [
      "DNA replicates through a process called **semiconservative replication**.",
      "Each new DNA molecule consists of **one original strand and one newly synthesized strand**.",
      "This mechanism ensures **accurate transmission** of genetic information from parent to daughter cells."
    ]
  },
  {
    "slide_number": 3,
    "title": "From DNA to Protein: The Central Dogma",
    "bullets": [
      "Genetic information flows from **DNA to RNA to protein**.",
      "This process begins with **transcription**, where DNA's code is copied into messenger RNA (mRNA).",
      "mRNA acts as an **intermediate messenger**, carrying the genetic instructions out of the nucleus."
    ]
  },
  {
    "slide_number": 4,
    "title": "mRNA's Role: Directing Protein Synthesis",
    "bullets": [
      "Once formed, **mRNA travels to the ribosomes** in the cytoplasm.",
