[
  {
    "slide_number": 1,
    "title": "Life's Fundamental Processes: Energy & Replication",
    "bullets": [
      "All living organisms require **energy** to function and the ability to **reproduce** to continue their species.",
      "We'll explore two core biological processes: **photosynthesis** (energy capture) and **cell division** (replication)."
    ]
  },
  {
    "slide_number": 2,
    "title": "Photosynthesis: Capturing Light Energy",
    "bullets": [
      "Photosynthesis converts light energy into chemical energy, primarily in plants and algae.",
      "The process occurs in two main stages within the chloroplast:",
      "â   **Light-dependent reactions** take place in the **grana** (stacks of thylakoids).",
      "â   **Light-independent reactions** (Calvin cycle) occur in the **stroma** (fluid-filled space)."
    ]
  },
  {
    "slide_number": 3,
    "title": "Cell Division: Ensuring Genetic Continuity",
    "bullets": [
      "Cell division is crucial for growth, repair, and reproduction in all living things.",
      "There are two primary types of cell division, each with a distinct outcome for chromosome number."
    ]
  },
  {
    "slide_number": 4,
    "title": "Mitosis vs. Meiosis: Different Goals for Chromosomes",
    "bullets": [
      "**Mitosis** is somatic cell division, resulting in **two identical daughter cells**.",
      "â   Its primary role is to **maintain the chromosome number** from the parent cell.",
      "**Meiosis** is germ cell division, producing **four genetically distinct daughter cells** (gametes).",
      "â   Its key function is to **halve the chromosome number**, preparing for sexual reproduction."
    ]
  },
  {
    "slide_number": 5,
    "title": "Conclusion: Interconnected Biological Foundations",
    "bullets": [
      "**Photosynthesis** provides the energy foundation for most ecosystems, converting light into usable chemical energy.",
      "**Cell division** ensures the continuity of life, whether through growth (mitosis) or sexual reproduction (meiosis).",
      "These processes, though distinct, are fundamental to the **survival and diversity of life** on Earth."
    ]
  }
]

